    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the earlier confusion; I was just trying to inject a bit of local folklore about the Peloponnese, but I understand you need a straight technical breakdown.\n\nTo finish explaining the **reducing atmosphere** specifically regarding iron oxide levels:\n\nIn a reducing environment, the lack of oxygen prevents the iron in the clay from oxidizing into hematite (Fe₂O₃), which is the reddish-brown pigment. Instead, the carbon monoxide (CO) and hydrogen (H₂) gases in the kiln atmosphere act as reducing agents. They strip the oxygen away from the iron, converting it into ferrous oxide (FeO) or metallic iron.\n\nThis chemical reduction results in two primary visual outcomes on your ceramic shards:\n1.  **Darkening:** The shards often turn a deep brown, black, or dark green because the iron is no longer reflecting light in the way oxidized iron does.\n2.  **Metallic Luster:** If the reduction is intense enough, you might see a distinct metallic sheen or a \"mottled\" appearance where the iron has been partially reduced to its metallic state, giving the surface a reflective quality that oxidizing fire cannot produce.",
